<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes> a slkg:Module ;
    rdfs:label "starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes" ;
    dcterms:identifier "starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes" ;
    rdfs:comment """SHACL shapes over the ``salg:`` vocabulary — structural validation for an
algebra graph (LLM-authored or hand-authored) *before* attempting
``from_rdf.rdf_to_query``/``rdf_to_update``.

Scope: full coverage as of the last update to this docstring (2026-08) —
core graph-pattern operators (``BGP``/``Filter``/``LeftJoin``/``Union``/
``Extend``/``Minus``/``ServiceGraphPattern``/``Join``/``ToMultiSet``), the
``TriplePattern``/``TripleTerm``/``Variable`` conventions, all five property
path types, ``VALUES``, aggregates/``GROUP BY`` (all 7 real
``Aggregate_*`` forms), ``Slice``/``Distinct``/``Reduced``/``OrderBy``,
every query form (``SELECT``/``CONSTRUCT``/``ASK``/``DESCRIBE``), full
SPARQL Update (all ten operations), and ``salg:QueryCollection``. Any new
operator needs its own shape added the same way — see
``salg:GraphPatternShape``'s ``sh:or`` list in ``sparql_shapes.ttl`` (loaded
below as ``SHAPES_TURTLE``), which is the one place a new operator's shape must be registered for it to
be accepted inside a nested ``p``/``p1``/``p2`` position. (This scope note
previously described only Phase 1 + VALUES/subqueries and had drifted well
out of date with the file's actual content by the time this was corrected
— if this note and the shape inventory below ever disagree again, trust
the code: grep for ``^salg:[A-Za-z_]*Shape$`` to get the real, current
list.)

Full expression-tree validation (``Filter``/``LeftJoin``/``Extend``/
``OrderCondition``'s ``expr``) is now done — all 63 of rdflib's expression
builtins (see ``from_rdf._discover_expr_evalfns``) have their own shape,
grouped into reusable argument-signature families; see
``salg:ExpressionShape`` and ``_EXPR_NODE_FAMILY`` below. One deliberate
exception: ``Builtin_EXISTS``/``Builtin_NOTEXISTS``'s ``.graph`` property
is a *raw, untranslated* SPARQL parse-tree fragment (rdflib evaluates
``EXISTS``/``NOT EXISTS`` lazily, translating it on demand at evaluation
time rather than at parse time) — a genuinely different vocabulary from
the translated-algebra shapes everything else here targets, confirmed by
inspecting real decoded algebra output; validating that separate
vocabulary is out of scope for this pass, so ``.graph`` is checked for
cardinality only, same as the still-open gaps above.

**Known limitation, shared with ``GraphPatternShape``: this file uses
``sh:class`` (a plain type-membership check), never ``sh:node``, inside
any dispatch ``sh:or`` list (``GraphPatternShape``, ``ExpressionShape``)
— never the reverse.** ``sh:node`` triggers full recursive shape
conformance and pushes onto pyshacl's own shape-evaluation stack;
``pyshacl``'s shape-recursion guard (``constraints/
constraint_component.py::recursion_triggers``) tracks *shape identity*
in that stack, not (shape, focus-node) pairs, so it cannot distinguish a
true data cycle from a merely-deep-but-finite tree — and confirmed
empirically to misfire on very shallow, everyday queries (not just
pathologically deep ones) when a dispatch shape's own alternatives loop
back to it via ``sh:node``. ``sh:class`` never touches that stack, so
this is safe regardless of query depth — see ``GraphPatternShape``'s own
comment in ``sparql_shapes.ttl`` for the full account, including how this
was actually discovered (a real, previously-undetected gap: ``Extend``,
``BIND``'s algebra node, was missing from ``GraphPatternShape`` entirely
and nothing caught it, because the old ``sh:node``-based dispatch was
silently giving up before it ever reached that check).

Unlike ``to_rdf.py``/``from_rdf.py``, these shapes are hand-authored, not
generated by introspecting rdflib — rdflib has no static schema of "which
keys does a BGP CompValue have" to introspect (they're just assembled ad
hoc inside ``algebra.py``'s translate functions), so there's no live
source of truth to mirror mechanically the way ``_discover_expr_evalfns``
mirrors the parser grammar.

**Known limitation: shape-graph recursion.** ``GraphPatternShape`` (the
"what's valid inside a nested ``p``/``p1``/``p2`` position" shape) refers
to itself indirectly through ``FilterShape``/``LeftJoinShape``/
``UnionShape`` — genuine recursion, since SPARQL patterns nest to
arbitrary depth. SHACL Core does not require conforming processors to
support recursive shapes at all (see
https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/#shapes-recursion), and pyshacl's concrete
behavior is: after ~3 revisits of the *same* shape in a row it silently
stops validating that branch and treats it as conformant (a
``ShapeRecursionWarning``, not an error) rather than raising. In practice
this means a query nesting the same operator (e.g. ``OPTIONAL`` inside
``OPTIONAL`` inside ``OPTIONAL``...) more than ~3 levels deep will not have
its deepest levels structurally checked — a false-negative risk, not a
false-positive one (it can miss a malformed deep node, it won't reject a
well-formed one). ``rdf:List`` traversal (``BGP.triples``) is *not*
subject to this, since ``TriplePatternListShape`` below is a single
SPARQL-based constraint rather than a recursively-self-referential
NodeShape, specifically to avoid this. Revisit if deep operator nesting
turns out to matter in practice - e.g. by moving the operator-alternation
check to a SPARQL-based constraint the same way the list shape was.""" ;
    slkg:belongsToPackage <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/package/starsparql> ;
    slkg:commentText "\"\"\"...\"\"\" delimiters no longer need Python-level backslash-escaping",
        "(SHAPES_TURTLE = \"\"\"...\"\"\", ~1700 lines) - moved to a standalone",
        "Python string), so it's directly usable by any RDF tool without going",
        "The hand-authored shapes below used to be a giant inline Python string",
        "already-established pattern (a real, directly-usable Turtle file, not a",
        "now that they are not nested inside a Python triple-quoted string.",
        "pragma: no cover - exercised only when pyshacl isn't installed",
        "sparql_shapes.ttl file 2026-08-16, mirroring salg-ontology.ttl's own",
        "through this module. Every embedded sh:select query body's own" ;
    slkg:contains <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes._generate_expression_shapes_turtle>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.shapes_graph>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.validate> ;
    slkg:defines <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes._generate_expression_shapes_turtle>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.shapes_graph>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.validate> ;
    slkg:filePath "packages/sparql/starsparql/ontology/sparql_shapes.py" ;
    slkg:importsModule <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/__future__>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/pathlib>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/pyshacl>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/pyshacl.errors>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/warnings>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.expr_families>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.ontology> ;
    slkg:moduleName "starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes" .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.serialize12> a slkg:Module ;
    rdfs:label "starsparql.serialize12" ;
    dcterms:identifier "starsparql.serialize12" ;
    rdfs:comment """Serialize a SPARQL 1.2 algebra tree (with ``TripleTermNode`` nodes) back
into SPARQL 1.2 query text — the counterpart to ``parse12.py``.

Extends rdflib's own ``algebra.translateAlgebra``/``_AlgebraTranslator``,
which already turns an ordinary (SPARQL-1.1-shaped) algebra tree back into
query text, in three ways:

1. ``BGP``/``TriplesBlock`` (the latter used for CONSTRUCT templates) call
   ``.n3()`` directly on each triple-pattern term rather than delegating
   through ``convert_node_arg``/child traversal the way most other
   operators do — confirmed by reading the real ``algebra.py`` source
   before relying on it. ``CompValue`` (and therefore ``TripleTermNode``)
   has no ``.n3()`` method, so both branches are overridden to render a
   ``TripleTermNode`` as ``<<( s p o )>>`` instead of crashing with
   ``AttributeError``.
2. ``Builtin_isTRIPLE``/``SUBJECT``/``PREDICATE``/``OBJECT`` are new SPARQL
   1.2 builtins rdflib has no branch for at all — added following the same
   pattern as any other single-argument builtin (e.g. ``Builtin_STR``).
3. ``ConstructQuery`` is a **new capability**, not a patch to an existing
   branch: confirmed empirically that plain, unmodified rdflib has *no*
   ``ConstructQuery`` case at all — ``translateAlgebra`` already returns an
   empty string for a CONSTRUCT query even with zero triple terms involved,
   since ``self._alg_translation`` is only ever seeded by the ``SelectQuery``
   branch. Two non-obvious things had to be gotten right, both found only by
   testing against a real execution target, not by reading source alone:
   (a) ``node.p`` (a ``Project`` wrapping the WHERE pattern) can't be
   referenced the way ``SelectQuery`` does — for CONSTRUCT, ``Project.PV``
   is internal bookkeeping (which variables the template needs), not "the
   variable list to print after SELECT", but the base class's ``Project``
   branch doesn't know that distinction and prints it anyway, producing
   invalid syntax (a stray ``?x`` between ``WHERE`` and ``{``) — fixed by
   referencing ``node.p.p`` directly, skipping ``Project``'s own branch
   entirely; (b) the literal ``WHERE`` keyword, though optional per the
   SPARQL grammar itself (confirmed: rdflib's own ``WhereClause`` production
   is ``Optional(Keyword("WHERE")) + ...``, and this project's SELECT
   serialization has always omitted it), is required for compatibility with
   *starlayergraph's* SPARQL-1.2-to-1.1 rewriter specifically — a regex-based,
   non-full-grammar-parsing tool (by its own docstring) confirmed to fail on
   a WHERE-less CONSTRUCT even though plain rdflib itself re-parses that
   same text without complaint.

Everything else is inherited from ``_AlgebraTranslator`` completely
unchanged.

Scope: Query algebra only (``SELECT``/``CONSTRUCT``), matching this
project's existing ``translateAlgebra`` usage — Update algebra was never
serialized back to text by this project either (see
``test_phase2_update.py``'s docstring: "this project doesn't attempt to
serialize Update algebra back to text at all"). ``ASK``/``DESCRIBE`` are
still unsupported — confirmed the same "no branch, nothing ever seeds
``self._alg_translation``" gap applies to them too, not investigated
further since nothing in this project's scope currently needs them. A
``VALUES`` row containing a triple-term *value* (as opposed to a triple
term appearing in an ordinary triple pattern) is not yet handled either —
rdflib's own ``values`` branch calls ``.n3()`` on each row value too, the
same class of gap, just not hit by any test yet. Worth fixing the same way
if either comes up.""" ;
    slkg:belongsToPackage <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/package/starsparql> ;
    slkg:contains <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/class/starsparql.serialize12._AlgebraTranslator12>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12._dirlang_n3>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12._term_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12._triples_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12.translate_algebra_12>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/method/starsparql.serialize12._AlgebraTranslator12.convert_node_arg>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/method/starsparql.serialize12._AlgebraTranslator12.sparql_query_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/method/starsparql.serialize12._AlgebraTranslator12.translateAlgebra> ;
    slkg:defines <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/class/starsparql.serialize12._AlgebraTranslator12>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12._dirlang_n3>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12._term_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12._triples_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.serialize12.translate_algebra_12> ;
    slkg:filePath "packages/sparql/starsparql/serialize12.py" ;
    slkg:importsModule <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/__future__>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.algebra>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.parserutils>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.sparql>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.term>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.triple_term>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.vocab> ;
    slkg:moduleName "starsparql.serialize12" .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.ssyn_to_text> a slkg:Module ;
    rdfs:label "starsparql.ssyn_to_text" ;
    dcterms:identifier "starsparql.ssyn_to_text" ;
    rdfs:comment """Render ``ssyn:`` RDF (``to_ssyn_rdf.py``'s output) back into literal
SPARQL query text — the completion of the round trip, closing it through
text rather than by hand-reconstructing algebra ``CompValue`` trees.

This is a much lower-risk path than driving ``rdflib.plugins.sparql.algebra
.translateGroupGraphPattern``/``translateQuery`` directly: ``ssyn:`` is
already syntax-shaped (flat WHERE-clause elements, real resolved terms), so
rendering it as text is close to mechanical — no reconstruction of
``TriplesBlock``/path/precedence-chain shapes needed at all, since none of
that verbosity was ever introduced on the encode side. Once we have text,
execution goes through this project's own real, already-tested pipeline
(``parse12.prepare_query_12`` / plain ``prepareQuery``) unchanged — nothing
new needed there either.

The one place this *does* reuse existing machinery rather than writing a
new renderer: ``ssyn:Filter``/``ssyn:Bind``'s ``ssyn:expr`` falls back to
the existing ``salg:`` expression vocabulary (see ``to_ssyn_rdf.py``'s
module docstring), and rendering *that* subtree as text reuses rdflib's own
``algebra.translateAlgebra`` — via a throwaway wrapper query
(``SELECT * WHERE { FILTER(<expr>) }``), since ``translateAlgebra`` only
ever seeds its output from a top-level query node (confirmed, see CLAUDE.md
finding #14) — rather than hand-writing a second expression-to-text
renderer duplicating ``_AlgebraTranslator``'s existing ~63-builtin
coverage.""" ;
    slkg:belongsToPackage <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/package/starsparql> ;
    slkg:contains <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._expr_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._predicate_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._render_expr_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._term_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._where_element_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._where_group_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text.ssyn_rdf_to_query_text> ;
    slkg:defines <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._expr_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._predicate_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._render_expr_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._term_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._where_element_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text._where_group_text>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ssyn_to_text.ssyn_rdf_to_query_text> ;
    slkg:filePath "packages/sparql/starsparql/ssyn_to_text.py" ;
    slkg:importsModule <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/__future__>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.collection>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.namespace>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.algebra>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.parserutils>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.sparql>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.from_rdf>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.ssyn_vocab>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.vocab> ;
    slkg:moduleName "starsparql.ssyn_to_text" .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.to_ast_rdf> a slkg:Module ;
    rdfs:label "starsparql.to_ast_rdf" ;
    dcterms:identifier "starsparql.to_ast_rdf" ;
    rdfs:comment """Encode a raw SPARQL parse tree (``rdflib.plugins.sparql.parser
.parseQuery``'s output, *before* ``translateQuery`` runs) as ``sast:`` RDF —
a prototype sibling to ``to_rdf.py``, one level lower (parse tree instead of
algebra).

Confirmed empirically (by directly inspecting ``parseQuery()``'s output)
that this needs almost no new machinery beyond what ``to_rdf.py`` already
established as a pattern:

- The tree is already overwhelmingly ``CompValue``-shaped, exactly like the
  algebra — same generic ``node.name`` -> ``rdf:type``, ``key: value`` ->
  predicate encoding works unchanged, just against grammar-production names
  (``TriplesBlock``, ``OptionalGraphPattern``, ``Filter``, ``PathAlternative``,
  ``ConditionalOrExpression``, ...) instead of algebra operator names.
- Property paths need *no* special-casing here at all (unlike ``to_rdf.py``'s
  dedicated ``_encode_path`` for ``rdflib.paths.Path`` objects) — at the
  parse-tree level a path is just an ordinary ``PathAlternative``/
  ``PathSequence``/``PathElt`` ``CompValue`` chain, already covered by the
  generic branch.
- The one real gap: a plain, unnamed ``pyparsing.ParseResults`` shows up on
  its own (not just as `CompValue`'s own storage) inside ``TriplesBlock
  .triples`` — a flat, *ungrouped* run of terms (e.g. ``[?p, path, :o,
  ?p, path2, ?x]`` for two semicolon-chained triples sharing a subject,
  always a multiple of 3 terms long — confirmed directly, not assumed).
  Grouping it into real ``(s, p, o)`` triples is *not* needed for a correct
  round trip: ``translateQuery`` groups it itself (that's exactly what it
  already does when called on a fresh parse straight from text), so this
  layer only needs to preserve the flat shape faithfully, encoded as an
  ordinary ``rdf:List`` — one new dispatch branch, not a new algorithm.""" ;
    slkg:belongsToPackage <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/package/starsparql> ;
    slkg:contains <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._build_rdf_list>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._encode>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._encode_comp_value>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._encode_list>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf.query_ast_to_rdf> ;
    slkg:defines <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._build_rdf_list>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._encode>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._encode_comp_value>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf._encode_list>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.to_ast_rdf.query_ast_to_rdf> ;
    slkg:filePath "packages/sparql/starsparql/to_ast_rdf.py" ;
    slkg:importsModule <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/__future__>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/pyparsing>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.collection>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.namespace>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/external/rdflib.plugins.sparql.parserutils>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.ast_vocab>,
        <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.to_rdf> ;
    slkg:moduleName "starsparql.to_ast_rdf" .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/module/starsparql.to_ssyn_rdf> a slkg:Module ;
    rdfs:label "starsparql.to_ssyn_rdf" ;
    dcterms:identifier "starsparql.to_ssyn_rdf" ;
    rdfs:comment """Encode a bare SELECT query's raw parse tree (``parseQuery()`` output,
same input ``to_ast_rdf.query_ast_to_rdf`` takes) as ``ssyn:`` — the
*readable* syntax-level RDF, no more verbose than the query text itself.

Everything that collapses the parse tree's native verbosity is a real,
existing rdflib function, reused directly, never reimplemented:

- ``algebra.translatePName`` (via ``traverse(..., visitPost=...)``) resolves
  prefixed names (``foaf:Person``) to real absolute IRIs — the parse tree
  keeps them as unresolved ``prefix``/``localname`` pairs otherwise.
- ``algebra.triples`` groups a ``TriplesBlock``'s flat, ungrouped run of
  terms into real ``(s, p, o)`` tuples (also reordering them — harmless
  here since a plain BGP's own triple order isn't semantically meaningful).
- ``algebra.translatePath`` collapses a trivial single-element
  ``PathAlternative``/``PathSequence``/``PathElt`` chain down to the bare
  predicate term, or builds a real ``rdflib.paths.Path`` object for a
  genuine path — reusing ``to_rdf._encode_path`` (unchanged) for the latter,
  the same salg:-boundary principle as expressions below.
- ``algebra.simplifyFilters`` collapses a ``ConditionalOrExpression ->
  ConditionalAndExpression -> ...`` precedence chain down to whatever real
  expression is actually present, whenever there's no genuine ``&&``/``||``.

A ``WHERE`` clause is encoded as a flat, ordered list of sibling elements —
mirroring ``GroupGraphPatternSub.part``'s own already-flat shape in the raw
parse tree — not the algebra's nested ``LeftJoin``/``Filter`` wrapping. See
``ssyn_vocab.py``'s module docstring for why.""" ;
