<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.validate> a slkg:Function ;
    rdfs:label "validate" ;
    rdfs:comment """Validate ``data_graph`` (e.g. from ``to_rdf.query_to_rdf``, or an
LLM-authored ``salg:`` graph not yet decoded) against the shapes above.

Runs with RDFS reasoning enabled (``ont_graph=ontology_graph()``,
``inference="rdfs"``) - this is what lets ``GraphPatternShape``/
``ExpressionShape``/``SubSelectShape`` be a single ``sh:class`` check
against an abstract superclass (``salg:GraphPattern``/``salg:Expression``/
``salg:SubSelect``) instead of enumerating every concrete
operator/builtin by name: with reasoning on, a node asserted as e.g.
``salg:Builtin_STR`` is *also* entailed to be ``rdf:type``
``salg:OneArgExpression`` and ``salg:Expression`` (the ontology's own
``rdfs:subClassOf`` chain), so ``sh:class salg:Expression`` alone
matches it - confirmed empirically, not assumed. Every individual
operator/builtin shape (``BGPShape``, ``Builtin_STRShape``, ...) is
unaffected either way - RDFS subclass reasoning only ever *adds*
broader inferred types to an already-narrowly-typed node, never
invents narrower ones, so ``sh:targetClass salg:Builtin_STR`` still
fires only for nodes actually asserted as that type.

Returns ``(conforms, results_graph, results_text)`` — the same shape
``pyshacl.validate`` itself returns, so a caller that wants the raw
SHACL validation report can use it directly.""" ;
    slkg:commentText "ExpressionShape/SubSelectShape) references another shape by name",
        "No shape in this file's dispatch shapes (GraphPatternShape/",
        "anymore (sh:class, not sh:node/sh:or-of-shapes - see each one's own",
        "comment in SHAPES_TURTLE), so pyshacl's shape-recursion guard",
        "shouldn't have anything left to trip on. Kept as a defensive",
        "suppression, not because it's expected to fire." ;
    slkg:hasParameter <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/parameter/https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fhidden-graph%2Fstarlayer%2Fkg%2Fresource%2Ffunction%2Fstarsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.validate%3A%3Adata_graph%3A%3A0> ;
    slkg:lineEnd 181 ;
    slkg:lineStart 141 ;
    slkg:qualifiedName "starsparql.ontology.sparql_shapes.validate" ;
    slkg:signature "def validate(data_graph: Graph) -> tuple[bool, Graph, str]" .

<https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/function/starsparql.parse12._canonicalize_construct_where> a slkg:Function ;
    rdfs:label "_canonicalize_construct_where" ;
    rdfs:comment """``CONSTRUCT WHERE { pattern }`` shorthand (the template IS the WHERE
pattern) reaches here with ``algebra.template`` set to ``None`` —
confirmed via plain, unmodified ``rdflib.plugins.sparql.algebra
.translateQuery``, not something this project's own grammar/algebra
work introduced. ``pattern`` itself is required by the SPARQL grammar
(``ConstructWhere`` — a plain ``TriplesTemplate``, no FILTER/OPTIONAL/
UNION/etc permitted) to be exactly a flat BGP, so ``algebra.p.p.triples``
is always exactly the triple list needed.

Populating ``template`` explicitly here — the *canonical*, always-
explicit-template form of a CONSTRUCT query — rather than leaving the
``None``/"use the WHERE clause" convention for downstream code to
rediscover is a deliberate simplification, not merely a serialization
workaround: this project's own RDF encoding of the algebra
(``to_rdf.py``) has no special "template is really the WHERE clause"
convention of its own, so leaving ``template=None`` here means that
information is silently lost the moment the algebra is encoded to RDF
- confirmed via the W3C ``construct-*``/``expr-1`` fixtures, which
regenerated as ``CONSTRUCT {}`` (a literal, always-empty-result
template) after a round trip through RDF, a real semantic
mistranslation, not just lost shorthand syntax. Regenerating the
*shorthand* ``CONSTRUCT WHERE`` spelling specifically (as opposed to an
equivalent explicit ``CONSTRUCT { ... } WHERE { ... }``) is explicitly
out of scope for now, per instruction - only the meaning needs to
survive, not the original surface syntax.""" ;
    slkg:commentText "(`alg.template` == `alg['template']`), but has no real __setattr__",
        "(rdflib.plugins.sparql.parserutils) is an OrderedDict subclass",
        "CompValue['template'] = ..., NOT alg.template = ... - CompValue",
        "anywhere query_to_rdf() could see it.",
        "bug this way: `alg.template` read back the assigned list",
        "correctly (plain attribute lookup finds the instance dict first),",
        "executed) - so a plain `alg.template = [...]` silently creates an",
        "loop (to_rdf.py) actually iterates over. Confirmed a real, silent",
        "ordinary shadow instance attribute instead of updating the dict",
        "override at runtime (only a `if TYPE_CHECKING:` stub, never",
        "query_to_rdf() showed zero results - the assignment never reached",
        "storage _encode_comp_value's own `for key, value in node.items()`",
        "while `graph.triples((root, SALG.template, None))` after",
        "whose __getattr__ routes attribute *reads* through to dict access" ;
    slkg:hasParameter <https://github.com/hidden-graph/starlayer/kg/resource/parameter/https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fhidden-graph%2Fstarlayer%2Fkg%2Fresource%2Ffunction%2Fstarsparql.parse12._canonicalize_construct_where%3A%3Aquery%3A%3A0> ;
    slkg:lineEnd 119 ;
    slkg:lineStart 75 ;
    slkg:qualifiedName "starsparql.parse12._canonicalize_construct_where" ;
    slkg:signature "def _canonicalize_construct_where(query: Query) -> Query" .

